completed the "awk for Data Processing" lab, where I gained hands-on experience with one of the most powerful and efficient text-processing tools available in Unix-like environments.
awk is a versatile pattern-scanning and processing language that excels at manipulating structured text data without needing to load everything into memory — making it ideal for log analysis, data extraction, reporting, and quick transformations on large files.
What I Mastered
1. Extracting Specific Columns
Learned how to precisely select and print desired fields from delimited data using field variables ($1, $2, etc.).
Example Command:
awk '{print $1, $3}' data.txt
This cleanly outputs the Name and Gender columns while ignoring the rest.
2. Filtering Rows with Conditions
Developed the ability to apply logical filters directly in the pattern section of awk.
Example Command:
awk '$2 > 25 {print $0}' data.txt
This returns only the records where Age is greater than 25, demonstrating conditional processing in a single, efficient pass.
Skills Gained

Understanding awk's core syntax: pattern { action }
Working with default field separators (whitespace) and the full record variable $0
Combining filtering and printing operations for targeted data extraction
Appreciating awk's strength for lightweight, fast, and scriptable data processing on the command line
